@title
Labeled Tuple Omission

@flags
show_report version=1 id=73bc65c8-0e53-478d-a74d-a0b0653674a9

@prompt
Entries can be dropped from a labeled tuple with `omit_labels`.

```hazel
let coordinate = (x=1, y=2, z=3) in
omit_labels(coordinate, `x`)
```

Labels are not strings, so they are delimited by backticks rather than double quotes to give them stronger typing.

Multiple labels can be provided as extra arguments:

```hazel
omit_labels((a=1, b=2, c=3, d=4), `a`, `c`)
```

# Task

Drop the `secret` field from the tuple:

```hazelnostatics
drop_secrets((not_secret="public information", secret="to redact"))
==
(not_secret="public information")
```

@hint
Remove the `secret` field

@reference
### Label Omission
Drop a single field by label:
```hazel
let coordinate = (x=1, y=2, z=3) in
omit_labels(coordinate, `x`)
```

---

Drop multiple fields by passing extra label arguments:
```hazel
omit_labels(
(a=1, b=2, c=3, d=4),
`a`,
`c`)
```

@hints
Example input
Don't specialize on the example but use omit_labels

@code
let drop_secrets = fun t -> 
¿
in
drop_secrets((not_secret="public information", secret="to redact"))

@test
test drop_secrets((not_secret="public information", secret="to redact")) == (not_secret="public information") end;
let t = (a=1, b=2, c=3, secret=123) in
test drop_secrets(t) == (a=1, b=2, c=3) end
